desc:Variable Ratio Compressor MB (Magnolius)
// @description Variable Ratio Compressor MB (Magnolius)
// @author Magnolius
// @version 1.2
// @link GitHub https://github.com/m-dahlberg/magnolius-reaper
// @provides
//   gui_kit/gui_kit.jsfx-inc
//   gui_kit/*.png
//   Magnolius_VariableRatioCompressorMB.help.txt
// @about
//   Four-band version of the Variable Ratio Compressor. Each band solves for
//   the ratio that lands its loudest upcoming peak on the ceiling, then holds
//   it across the passage.
//
//   The crossover is a sequential LR4 tree with allpass compensation, so the
//   four bands sum flat - flat magnitude, phase rotation only.
//
//   Note that the ceiling is per band, and four bands each held at the ceiling
//   can sum above it: the ceiling is a shaping control here, not the output
//   level. Use Output Gain for level. A disabled band still runs its delay
//   line, so toggling one never shifts the others in time.
//
//   Licensed GPL-3.0-or-later.
// @changelog
//   New interface built on the Magnolius GUI kit, matching the single-band
//   plugin: knobs with typed value fields, the scrolling display in a framed
//   plot, four per-band lanes under it, live transfer curves for all four
//   bands, GR and ratio meters, a scale selector shared with the other
//   Magnolius plugins, and a built-in help panel.
//
//   The scrolling display now plots dB rather than linear amplitude, with the
//   ceiling drawn on it as a real reference line, and each band's enable, solo
//   and gain reduction are on the band's own panel rather than in a list of
//   sliders.
//
//   The v1.2 simplifications from the single band are all here now:
//
//   Max Ratio runs 1:1 to 10:1 in steps of 0.1, default 3, instead of 2:1 to
//   1000:1 in steps of 1 defaulting to 200. A project saved above 10 loads
//   clamped to 10 and will compress less than it used to - lower the
//   thresholds or the ceiling to compensate. The ratio traces and the RATIO
//   meter are on a fixed 1:1 to 10:1 scale, so a given ratio always draws at
//   the same height.
//
//   Safety Clip and Trim Position are gone. Output Trim is now Output Gain,
//   always applied last, after the sum and outside every band's ratio solve.
//   A project saved in Pre will be louder and less compressed than it was.
//   Without the clipper the output is no longer bounded at 0 dBFS - put a
//   limiter after this if you need one.
//
//   New Ceiling Tilt (dB/oct). The ceiling is now a line across log frequency
//   rather than one number: each band takes its value at the band's geometric
//   centre, pivoted so the Ceiling knob stays the average of the four. At the
//   default 0 it is exactly the old fixed ceiling. Tilt it down and the upper
//   bands are held lower, which is what makes the top band do anything at all
//   on material whose spectrum falls with frequency - with a flat ceiling its
//   peaks rarely reach the target and the band sits idle. The curve panel
//   draws the four ceilings in the bands' own tints as soon as tilt is not 0.
//
//   Each band's threshold is now clamped to at least 0.1 dB below that band's
//   ceiling, as the single-band plugin has always done. A steep tilt can drop
//   a high band's ceiling past a threshold that was fine at tilt 0.
//
//   New Display History parameter (1-30 s) for the scroll window, which used
//   to be hardwired to about 5 s. It has no knob - the bottom row is three
//   panels now, and the space went to Tilt - but it is still automatable and
//   still reachable from REAPER's parameter list.
//
//   The bottom row is three panels rather than four: CROSSOVER, a wider
//   DYNAMICS holding Ceiling, Tilt, Max Ratio and Lookahead, and a wider GAIN
//   that also carries the GR and RATIO meters.
//
//   The B1-B4 labels under the curve plot are switches: clicking one hides or
//   shows that band's curve, so two curves can be compared without the other
//   two in the way. All four start shown, and the state is saved with the
//   project rather than exposed as a parameter.
//
//   Alt on any of the three per-band controls moves ALL FOUR bands by the same
//   amount, so a balance you have already dialled in survives the move.
//   Threshold and Gain step by a difference in dB; Release scales by a ratio,
//   since what relates 400 ms to 80 ms is a factor rather than a number of
//   milliseconds. The four move as one rigid body and stop when the first band
//   reaches an end of its range, including the one under the mouse, so the
//   offsets can never be flattened against a limit.
//
//   Alt-drag on Input Gain or Output Gain moves the other one the opposite way
//   by the same number of dB, so you can change how hard the bands work
//   without changing the level. It works on the knob, the wheel and the value
//   field, and each value still clamps to its own range.
//
//   Slider numbers 8 and 9 are left as GAPS rather than renumbering the rest
//   down, so every remaining control still loads into itself from an old
//   project. REAPER does compact the PARAMETER indices it exposes to
//   automation, so an existing envelope on Lookahead, Max Ratio, Listen or any
//   band control now points two parameters too high and needs re-pointing.
//   That is unavoidable when a parameter is removed.

// Four-band version of the adaptive-ratio compressor. Each band runs the same
// core: lookahead sliding-max finds the loudest upcoming peak, solves for the
// ratio that lands it on the ceiling, holds that ratio across the passage, then
// a sliding-min + double-boxcar guarantees no overshoot.
//
// CROSSOVER
// Sequential LR4 tree with allpass compensation, so the four bands sum flat:
//   x --LR4(f1)--> L1                                  -> AP(f2) -> AP(f3) -> band 1
//              \-> H1 --LR4(f2)--> L2                  -> AP(f3) -> band 2
//                              \-> H2 --LR4(f3)--> L3  ->           band 3
//                                              \-> H3  ->           band 4
// LR4 lowpass + highpass sums to a 2nd-order allpass at the crossover, so the
// compensating filters are plain Q=0.707 allpass biquads at f2 and f3. The sum
// is AP(f1)*AP(f2)*AP(f3): flat magnitude, phase rotation only.
//
// CEILING
// Ceiling is the per-band target and is shared by all four bands. Four bands
// each held at the ceiling can SUM above it, so the ceiling is a shaping
// control, not the output level -- use Output Gain for that. As of 1.2 there
// is no safety clipper behind any of this.
//
// A disabled band still runs its delay line, so bands stay sample-aligned and
// toggling one never shifts the others in time.
//
// THREADING - do not undo this.  Every @gfx variable is g_-prefixed and no
// name is used on both sides.  The rule is not "@gfx calls no functions": it
// is that NO FUNCTION IS CALLED FROM BOTH THREADS.  EEL2 function locals AND
// parameters are static and shared, so a function entered from @gfx while the
// audio thread is inside it stomps the audio thread's copy of every local
// mid-sample.  So:
//   audio thread only:  bq, bq_*, split_*, band_*, lin2db, update_params
//   @gfx only:          accent, ui_scale, count_missing_images, g_fknob,
//                       g_curve, every gk_*
// That is also why the transfer curves inline the compressor maths instead of
// sharing a function with @sample, and why a widget change sets need_update
// rather than calling update_params() from the UI thread - @block picks the
// flag up on the audio thread, so the new coefficients do not depend on REAPER
// re-running @slider after slider_automate (which it does not).
//
// The same reasoning runs the other way for DRAWING.  @block does not run on a
// stopped track, so @gfx must not read the audio thread's derived values or
// every reference line, every band header and the whole curve panel freeze
// until the user presses play.  @gfx therefore derives g_ceil / g_blend and the
// three ordered crossover frequencies from the raw slider variables itself, at
// the top of the section.  Those lines duplicate update_params() on purpose -
// keep them in step.  The meters are the exception: they are audio-thread
// readouts by definition and do freeze when the transport stops.
//
// EEL2 identifiers are case-insensitive: W and w are ONE variable.  The
// detector window length is the global W, so nothing in @gfx may use a bare
// w - hence the g_ prefix on every last one of them.
//
// v1.2

// Shared memory, namespaced to this vendor so it cannot collide with other
// plugins' gmem. Slot map (keep in step across every options:gmem=Magnolius
// plugin - they share the one map):
//   gmem[0]  interface scale, as index+1 (0 = not set yet)
options:gmem=Magnolius

// Every slider name carries a leading '-' so REAPER's generic control panel
// stays hidden behind the custom interface, while the parameters remain
// automatable and visible to the render tests.
slider1:s_in=0<-12,24,0.1>-Input Gain (dB)

slider2:s_x1=120<20,500,1>-Crossover 1 (Hz)
slider3:s_x2=800<200,3000,1>-Crossover 2 (Hz)
slider4:s_x3=5000<1500,16000,10>-Crossover 3 (Hz)

slider5:s_ceil=-6<-36,0,0.05>-Ceiling (dB, per band)
slider6:s_blend=100<0,100,0.1>-Blend (% wet)
slider7:s_trim=0<-20,20,0.1>-Output Gain (dB)
// slider8 was Trim Position and slider9 was Safety Clip. Both are gone, and
// their NUMBERS ARE LEFT AS GAPS on purpose: a JSFX project stores slider
// values positionally, so renumbering 10-12 down into the holes would load an
// old project's Lookahead into Trim Position and so on, silently.
slider10:s_la=5<0.5,20,0.1>-Lookahead (ms)
slider11:s_maxr=3<1,10,0.1>-Max Ratio (:1)
slider12:s_listen=0<0,4,1{All bands,Band 1 only,Band 2 only,Band 3 only,Band 4 only}>-Listen
// Display History has no on-screen control - the panel space went to Ceiling
// Tilt - but it stays a parameter, so a project that set it keeps its window
// and it can still be reached from REAPER's parameter list.
slider13:s_hist=6<1,30,0.5>-Display History (s)

slider14:s_en1=1<0,1,1{Off,On}>-Band 1 Enable
slider15:s_th1=-18<-60,0,0.1>-Band 1 Threshold (dB)
slider16:s_rl1=400<10,2000,1>-Band 1 Ratio Release (ms)
slider17:s_gn1=0<-20,20,0.1>-Band 1 Gain (dB)

// Ceiling Tilt lives on 18 - a group separator no version of this plugin has
// ever written - rather than in the 8/9 gaps, where an old project's Trim
// Position or Safety Clip value would silently load as a tilt.
slider18:s_tilt=0<-6,6,0.1>-Ceiling Tilt (dB/oct)

slider19:s_en2=1<0,1,1{Off,On}>-Band 2 Enable
slider20:s_th2=-18<-60,0,0.1>-Band 2 Threshold (dB)
slider21:s_rl2=250<10,2000,1>-Band 2 Ratio Release (ms)
slider22:s_gn2=0<-20,20,0.1>-Band 2 Gain (dB)

slider24:s_en3=1<0,1,1{Off,On}>-Band 3 Enable
slider25:s_th3=-18<-60,0,0.1>-Band 3 Threshold (dB)
slider26:s_rl3=150<10,2000,1>-Band 3 Ratio Release (ms)
slider27:s_gn3=0<-20,20,0.1>-Band 3 Gain (dB)

slider29:s_en4=1<0,1,1{Off,On}>-Band 4 Enable
slider30:s_th4=-18<-60,0,0.1>-Band 4 Threshold (dB)
slider31:s_rl4=80<10,2000,1>-Band 4 Ratio Release (ms)
slider32:s_gn4=0<-20,20,0.1>-Band 4 Gain (dB)

in_pin:left
in_pin:right
out_pin:left
out_pin:right

import gui_kit/gui_kit.jsfx-inc

// A filename: line takes the WHOLE rest of the line as the path, so a trailing
// // comment becomes part of the filename and the image silently fails to load
// (the slot just reports 0 x 0). Keep these lines bare.
//   0 knob, band dynamics (cyan)      1 knob, gain (yellow)
//   2 knob, gain bipolar (per-band Gain, Output Gain)
//   13 knob, crossover and global dynamics (orange)
//   14 knob, orange bipolar (Ceiling Tilt)
//   12 band-enable LED                15 help text, last slot
filename:0,gui_kit/knob_cyan_grid.png
filename:1,gui_kit/knob_yellow_grid.png
filename:2,gui_kit/knob_yellow_bipolar_grid.png
filename:3,gui_kit/value_field.png
filename:4,gui_kit/header_bar.png
filename:5,gui_kit/panel_tile.png
filename:6,gui_kit/dropdown.png
filename:7,gui_kit/button_cyan.png
filename:8,gui_kit/wave_frame_9slice.png
filename:9,gui_kit/playhead_line_cyan.png
filename:10,gui_kit/playhead_marker_cyan.png
filename:11,gui_kit/logo_magnolius_grey.png
filename:12,gui_kit/led_cyan.png
filename:13,gui_kit/knob_orange_grid.png
filename:14,gui_kit/knob_orange_bipolar_grid.png
filename:15,Magnolius_VariableRatioCompressorMB.help.txt

@init

// @init re-runs on samplerate change and on recompile.  ext_noinit stops the
// other trigger - transport start - so the detector deques, the delay lines and
// the scrolling history survive hitting play instead of being wiped every time.
ext_noinit = 1;

DMAX    = 4096;                  // power of two; 20 ms of lookahead up to 200 kHz
MASK    = DMAX - 1;
BANDSZ  = DMAX * 8;
LN10_20 = 0.11512925464970229;   // ln(10)/20   dB -> linear exponent
DB20    = 8.6858896380650366;    // 20/ln(10)   ln  -> dB
TINY    = 10^(-30);              // EEL2 has no scientific notation: 1e-30 is a
                                 // silent syntax error that kills the section
BQQ     = 0.70710678118654752;   // Butterworth Q, for both LR4 and the allpasses

function lin2db(l) ( log(max(l, TINY)) * DB20 );

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- biquads --

function bq(x) instance(a0, a1, a2, b1, b2, z1, z2) local(y) (
  y  = x * a0 + z1;
  z1 = x * a1 - b1 * y + z2;
  z2 = x * a2 - b2 * y;
  y;
);

function bq_lp(fc, q) instance(a0, a1, a2, b1, b2) local(w, cs, sn, al, n0) (
  w = 2 * $pi * min(fc, srate * 0.45) / srate;
  cs = cos(w); sn = sin(w); al = sn / (2 * q); n0 = 1 + al;
  a0 = (1 - cs) * 0.5 / n0;
  a1 = (1 - cs) / n0;
  a2 = a0;
  b1 = -2 * cs / n0;
  b2 = (1 - al) / n0;
);

function bq_hp(fc, q) instance(a0, a1, a2, b1, b2) local(w, cs, sn, al, n0) (
  w = 2 * $pi * min(fc, srate * 0.45) / srate;
  cs = cos(w); sn = sin(w); al = sn / (2 * q); n0 = 1 + al;
  a0 = (1 + cs) * 0.5 / n0;
  a1 = -(1 + cs) / n0;
  a2 = a0;
  b1 = -2 * cs / n0;
  b2 = (1 - al) / n0;
);

function bq_ap(fc, q) instance(a0, a1, a2, b1, b2) local(w, cs, sn, al, n0) (
  w = 2 * $pi * min(fc, srate * 0.45) / srate;
  cs = cos(w); sn = sin(w); al = sn / (2 * q); n0 = 1 + al;
  a0 = (1 - al) / n0;
  a1 = -2 * cs / n0;
  a2 = 1;
  b1 = -2 * cs / n0;
  b2 = (1 - al) / n0;
);

// ------------------------------------------------------- crossover (1 ch) --

function split_setup(f1, f2, f3) (
  this.l1a.bq_lp(f1, BQQ); this.l1b.bq_lp(f1, BQQ);
  this.h1a.bq_hp(f1, BQQ); this.h1b.bq_hp(f1, BQQ);
  this.l2a.bq_lp(f2, BQQ); this.l2b.bq_lp(f2, BQQ);
  this.h2a.bq_hp(f2, BQQ); this.h2b.bq_hp(f2, BQQ);
  this.l3a.bq_lp(f3, BQQ); this.l3b.bq_lp(f3, BQQ);
  this.h3a.bq_hp(f3, BQQ); this.h3b.bq_hp(f3, BQQ);
  this.ap12.bq_ap(f2, BQQ);   // band 1 compensation
  this.ap13.bq_ap(f3, BQQ);
  this.ap23.bq_ap(f3, BQQ);   // band 2 compensation
);

function split_run(x) instance(b1, b2, b3, b4) local(lo, hi, lo2, hi2) (
  lo  = this.l1b.bq(this.l1a.bq(x));
  hi  = this.h1b.bq(this.h1a.bq(x));
  lo2 = this.l2b.bq(this.l2a.bq(hi));
  hi2 = this.h2b.bq(this.h2a.bq(hi));
  b3  = this.l3b.bq(this.l3a.bq(hi2));
  b4  = this.h3b.bq(this.h3a.bq(hi2));
  b1  = this.ap13.bq(this.ap12.bq(lo));
  b2  = this.ap23.bq(lo2);
);

// ------------------------------------------------------- band compressor --

function band_setup(base)
  instance(dlyL, dlyR, mxv, mxi, mnv, mni, ma1, ma2) (
  dlyL = base;
  dlyR = base + DMAX;
  mxv  = base + DMAX * 2;
  mxi  = base + DMAX * 3;
  mnv  = base + DMAX * 4;
  mni  = base + DMAX * 5;
  ma1  = base + DMAX * 6;
  ma2  = base + DMAX * 7;
);

function band_reset()
  instance(dlyL, ma1, ma2, sum1, sum2, b1p, b2p,
           mxh, mxt, mxc, mnh, mnt, mnc, n, wp, rp, rs, gs) (
  memset(dlyL, 0, DMAX * 2);          // dlyL and dlyR are contiguous
  memset(ma1, 1, DMAX);
  memset(ma2, 1, DMAX);
  sum1 = box; sum2 = box;
  b1p = 0; b2p = 0;
  mxh = 0; mxt = 0; mxc = 0;
  mnh = 0; mnt = 0; mnc = 0;
  n = 0; wp = 0; rp = DMAX - la;
  rs = 1; gs = 1;
);

function band_run(l, r, en, thr_db, rc, cl)
  instance(dlyL, dlyR, mxv, mxi, mnv, mni, ma1, ma2,
           wp, rp, n, mxh, mxt, mxc, mnh, mnt, mnc,
           b1p, b2p, sum1, sum2, rs, gs, outL, outR, inL, inR)
  local(a, pk, pk_db, greq, gdb, den, rden, rt, a_db, g, gm, s1) (

  dlyL[wp] = l;
  dlyR[wp] = r;
  a = max(abs(l), abs(r));

  // sliding maximum over the lookahead window
  while (mxc > 0 && mxv[(mxt + MASK) & MASK] <= a) ( mxt = (mxt + MASK) & MASK; mxc -= 1; );
  mxv[mxt] = a; mxi[mxt] = n; mxt = (mxt + 1) & MASK; mxc += 1;
  while (mxi[mxh] <= n - W) ( mxh = (mxh + 1) & MASK; mxc -= 1; );
  pk = mxv[mxh];

  // ratio that lands the upcoming peak exactly on THIS BAND'S ceiling (cl,
  // linear - the tilted per-band value).  Blend is solved for, not bolted on
  // afterwards; Output Gain is NOT, since it is applied once to the sum,
  // after everything.
  rt = 1;
  (en && pk > 0.0000001) ? (
    pk_db = log(pk) * DB20;
    pk_db > thr_db ? (
      greq = blend > 0.0001 ? (cl / pk - (1 - blend)) / blend : 2;
      greq < 1 ? (
        greq < 0.000001 ? greq = 0.000001;
        gdb  = log(greq) * DB20;
        den  = pk_db - thr_db;
        rden = 1 + gdb / den;
        rt   = rden > 0.000001 ? 1 / rden : maxr;
        rt > maxr ? rt = maxr;
        rt < 1    ? rt = 1;
      );
    );
  );

  rt > rs ? rs = rt : rs = rt + (rs - rt) * rc;

  g = 1;
  (en && a > 0.0000001) ? (
    a_db = log(a) * DB20;
    a_db > thr_db ? ( g = exp( -(a_db - thr_db) * (1 - 1/rs) * LN10_20 ); );
  );

  // sliding minimum of the gain, then two boxcars
  while (mnc > 0 && mnv[(mnt + MASK) & MASK] >= g) ( mnt = (mnt + MASK) & MASK; mnc -= 1; );
  mnv[mnt] = g; mni[mnt] = n; mnt = (mnt + 1) & MASK; mnc += 1;
  while (mni[mnh] <= n - W) ( mnh = (mnh + 1) & MASK; mnc -= 1; );
  gm = mnv[mnh];

  b1p += 1; b1p >= box ? b1p = 0;
  sum1 += gm - ma1[b1p]; ma1[b1p] = gm;
  s1 = sum1 / box;

  b2p += 1; b2p >= box ? b2p = 0;
  sum2 += s1 - ma2[b2p]; ma2[b2p] = s1;
  gs = sum2 / box;
  gs > 1 ? gs = 1;
  gs < 0 ? gs = 0;

  inL = dlyL[rp];
  inR = dlyR[rp];

  en ? (
    outL = inL * (1 - blend) + inL * gs * blend;
    outR = inR * (1 - blend) + inR * gs * blend;
  ) : (
    outL = inL; outR = inR;          // bypassed, but still delayed
  );

  wp += 1; wp >= DMAX ? wp = 0;
  rp += 1; rp >= DMAX ? rp = 0;
  n  += 1;
);

// ------------------------------------------------------------- allocation --

bnd1.band_setup(0 * BANDSZ);
bnd2.band_setup(1 * BANDSZ);
bnd3.band_setup(2 * BANDSZ);
bnd4.band_setup(3 * BANDSZ);

// ---- scrolling display history ----
// One column per col_len samples: the peak dB of the (delayed, time-aligned)
// summed input and of the output, plus each band's deepest gain reduction and
// highest ratio in that column.  @gfx only reads; @sample only writes.
// Power of two so the ring index is a mask, not a modulo.
HIST_N   = 1024;
HMASK    = HIST_N - 1;
HIST_IN  = 4 * BANDSZ;
HIST_OUT = HIST_IN  + HIST_N;
HIST_GR  = HIST_OUT + HIST_N;        // 4 * HIST_N, band-major
HIST_RT  = HIST_GR  + 4 * HIST_N;    // 4 * HIST_N

// Per-band scalars, band-indexed 0..3.
ben   = HIST_RT + 4 * HIST_N;        // enable
bthr  = ben   + 4;                   // threshold dB
brelc = bthr  + 4;                   // ratio release coefficient
bgain = brelc + 4;                   // band gain, linear
bmtr  = bgain + 4;                   // GR meter, linear, audio thread
brmt  = bmtr  + 4;                   // ratio meter, audio thread
bgrdb = brmt  + 4;                   // GR meter in dB, written by @block
accG  = bgrdb + 4;                   // column accumulator, min gain
accR  = accG  + 4;                   // column accumulator, max ratio
bcdb  = accR  + 4;                   // this band's ceiling in dB, after tilt
bclin = bcdb  + 4;                   // the same, linear, for the solver
fcb   = bclin + 4;                   // band centre frequency, geometric

// Display scales.  All three are fixed on purpose: a scale that followed a
// slider would make the same event draw a different size as the slider moved,
// which reads as the event having changed when it has not.
FLOORDB    = -66;            // waveform plot: centre line, 0 dB at the edges
GR_FULL_DB = 24;             // gain-reduction overlays, lanes and meter
RATIO_FULL = 10;             // ratio traces and meter (1:1 at the bottom).
                             // This MUST match slider11's maximum or the trace
                             // stops short of the top, or clips against it,
                             // with no error anywhere.
LN_RATIO   = log(RATIO_FULL);
// Nominal outer edges of the audible range, used only to give bands 1 and 4 a
// geometric centre frequency - they are open-ended in the DSP.
FC_LO      = 20;
FC_HI      = 20000;

CURVE_LO   = -60;            // transfer-curve panel, dB in
CURVE_HI   = 12;             // transfer-curve panel, dB out headroom

lastla     = -1;
need_reset = 1;
mtr        = 1;
rmtr       = 1;

// Display readouts, converted once per block rather than per sample, so @gfx
// never has to call lin2db (see the threading note).
gr_db  = 0;
rt_now = 1;

// Set by @gfx when a widget moves a slider, consumed by @block on the audio
// thread.  Never call update_params() from @gfx.
need_update = 0;

// Everything derived from the sliders lives here so @slider and @block cannot
// disagree, and so behaviour never depends on REAPER re-running @slider after
// slider_automate.  Idempotent, and audio thread only.
function update_params()
(
  ig       = exp(s_in * LN10_20);
  blend    = s_blend * 0.01;
  maxr     = s_maxr;

  // Output Gain is applied once, to the sum, after the bands and outside
  // every ratio solve - so it moves the level and nothing else.
  trim = exp(s_trim * LN10_20);

  // keep the crossovers ordered and inside Nyquist.  @gfx repeats these six
  // lines for its own display copies; keep the two in step.
  f1 = s_x1;
  f2 = max(s_x2, f1 * 1.1);
  f3 = max(s_x3, f2 * 1.1);
  f3 = min(f3, srate * 0.45);
  f2 = min(f2, f3 / 1.1);
  f1 = min(f1, f2 / 1.1);

  L.split_setup(f1, f2, f3);
  R.split_setup(f1, f2, f3);

  // ---- ceiling tilt ----
  // The ceiling is a line across log frequency, s_tilt dB per octave, and each
  // band takes its value at the band's GEOMETRIC CENTRE. The pivot is the
  // geometric mean of those four centres, so the Ceiling knob stays the
  // average of the four and tilting only redistributes: the sum of the four
  // offsets is exactly zero at any tilt. That is what makes tilt 0 identical
  // to the old fixed ceiling, and why turning it does not move the overall
  // level much. A band whose tilted ceiling would sit above 0 dBFS simply
  // stops compressing, which is what a ceiling that high means anyway.
  // @gfx repeats this maths for the display; keep the two in step.
  fcb[0] = sqrt(FC_LO * f1);
  fcb[1] = sqrt(f1 * f2);
  fcb[2] = sqrt(f2 * f3);
  fcb[3] = sqrt(f3 * min(FC_HI, srate * 0.5));
  fpiv = exp((log(fcb[0]) + log(fcb[1]) + log(fcb[2]) + log(fcb[3])) * 0.25);

  bi = 0;
  loop(4,
    bcdb[bi]  = s_ceil + s_tilt * log(fcb[bi] / fpiv) / log(2);
    bclin[bi] = exp(bcdb[bi] * LN10_20);
    bi += 1;
  );

  la = floor(s_la * 0.001 * srate + 0.5);
  la < 1 ? la = 1;
  la > DMAX - 8 ? la = DMAX - 8;
  W   = la + 1;                        // detector window length
  box = max(1, floor(la * 0.5));       // two cascaded boxcars, total span ~la

  ben[0]  = s_en1; ben[1]  = s_en2; ben[2]  = s_en3; ben[3]  = s_en4;
  // A threshold must stay below its band's ceiling, or the solve is asked for
  // a gain that makes no sense (the single-band plugin clamps the same way).
  // With a steep tilt a high band's ceiling can drop past a threshold that was
  // fine at tilt 0, so this is not a corner case.
  bthr[0] = min(s_th1, bcdb[0] - 0.1); bthr[1] = min(s_th2, bcdb[1] - 0.1);
  bthr[2] = min(s_th3, bcdb[2] - 0.1); bthr[3] = min(s_th4, bcdb[3] - 0.1);
  brelc[0] = exp(-1 / max(1, s_rl1 * 0.001 * srate));
  brelc[1] = exp(-1 / max(1, s_rl2 * 0.001 * srate));
  brelc[2] = exp(-1 / max(1, s_rl3 * 0.001 * srate));
  brelc[3] = exp(-1 / max(1, s_rl4 * 0.001 * srate));

  // Per-band gain, applied to the band output after compression and before the
  // sum. Like Output Gain it deliberately sits outside the ratio solve, so it
  // lifts the band past the ceiling rather than driving the compressor harder.
  bgain[0] = exp(s_gn1 * LN10_20);
  bgain[1] = exp(s_gn2 * LN10_20);
  bgain[2] = exp(s_gn3 * LN10_20);
  bgain[3] = exp(s_gn4 * LN10_20);

  pdc_delay  = la;
  pdc_bot_ch = 0;
  pdc_top_ch = 2;
  ext_tail_size = la + srate * 0.5;

  col_len = max(1, floor(srate * s_hist / HIST_N));

  la != lastla ? ( lastla = la; need_reset = 1; );

  need_reset ? (
    bnd1.band_reset(); bnd2.band_reset();
    bnd3.band_reset(); bnd4.band_reset();

    memset(HIST_IN, FLOORDB - 200, HIST_N * 2);   // IN and OUT are contiguous
    memset(HIST_GR, 0, HIST_N * 4);
    memset(HIST_RT, 1, HIST_N * 4);
    memset(bmtr, 1, 4); memset(brmt, 1, 4); memset(bgrdb, 0, 4);
    memset(accG, 1, 4); memset(accR, 1, 4);
    mtr = 1; rmtr = 1;
    hpos = 0; acc_in = 0; acc_out = 0; acc_n = 0;

    need_reset = 0;
  );
);

update_params();

// ==========================================================================
//  Interface
// ==========================================================================
gk_init();
gk_img_field = 3; gk_img_header = 4; gk_img_panel = 5;
gk_img_dropdown = 6; gk_img_button = 7;
gk_img_wave_frame = 8; gk_img_playhead_line = 9; gk_img_playhead_marker = 10;
IMG_LED = 12; IMG_KNOB_ORANGE = 13; IMG_KNOB_ORANGE_BI = 14;

// REAPER refuses to load an image taller than somewhere between 6144 and 7680
// px, and returns a zero-size image rather than an error - a filmstrip widget
// then draws nothing while still responding to the mouse.  Width is not
// affected.  So the knob strips are packed as grids: 64 frames in 8 columns
// (1024 x 1024).  The count lives in two places, here and in the PNG; change
// one without the other and the knob draws the wrong frame, silently.
gk_knob_cols = 8; gk_knob_frames = 64;

// Counts image slots REAPER would not load.  It reports a zero-size image and
// no error, and every filmstrip widget then falls back to vector drawing, so
// without this a failed load is completely silent and passes for working art.
// By far the most likely cause is a bad path - and note that a filename: line
// takes the whole rest of the line, so a trailing comment silently breaks it.
// @gfx only.
function count_missing_images(cnt) local(i, iw, ih, bad)
(
  i = 0; bad = 0;
  loop(cnt, gfx_getimgdim(i, iw, ih); iw <= 0 ? bad += 1; i += 1;);
  bad;
);

// Section accent, applied to everything the kit draws with primitives.  It
// persists between frames, so each section sets it before drawing.  @gfx only.
function accent(ar, ag, ab) ( gk_ac_r = ar; gk_ac_g = ag; gk_ac_b = ab; );

// ---- help viewer ----
// The panel is drawn by the widget library; the plugin only owns the state.
// The help file is the last filename: slot, after the images, so the
// missing-image count is not thrown off by it.
HELP_FILE = 15;
HELP_BASE = 300;          // first string slot used for help text
HELP_MAX  = 600;          // most lines kept
help_open = 0; help_lines = 0;

// ---- curve visibility ----
// One bit per band, all four shown by default. Clicking a B1..B4 legend label
// toggles its bit. GUI-only state: it is serialised, never automated.
curve_mask = 15;

// ---- interface scale ----
// The layout is authored at UI_W x UI_H logical px and every coordinate runs
// through gk_S, so scaling the whole interface - drawing, blits, hit-testing
// and drag deltas alike - is a matter of setting gk_S.  JSFX cannot resize its
// own window (@gfx W H is a compile-time literal and gfx_w/gfx_h are
// read-only), so this scales the content inside whatever window REAPER gives
// us; "Fit" tracks the window, a fixed percentage does not.  Keep UI_W/UI_H in
// step with the @gfx line: 986x668 is 100%, so 125% wants 1233x835 of window
// and 150% wants 1479x1002.
UI_W = 986; UI_H = 668;
LOGO_ASPECT = 512/101;    // logo_magnolius_grey.png
GM_UI_SCALE = 0;          // gmem slot holding the shared scale

// The scale is global: every instance reads it from gmem each frame, so
// changing it in one window changes all open windows and any instance added
// later in this session.  Each instance also serialises its own copy, which
// seeds the global when the first one loads in a fresh REAPER session.
// gmem is session-scoped: REAPER frees it once the last instance is removed,
// and a JSFX cannot write a settings file, so the scale cannot be persisted
// from in here.  100%, not Fit: the @gfx line already asks for exactly the
// design size, so 100% is pixel-exact on a fresh window and the artwork is
// blitted 1:1, where Fit softens everything by a fractional factor as soon as
// REAPER restores the window at some other size.  Keep this in step across
// every options:gmem=Magnolius plugin - whichever opens first in a session
// seeds the shared slot.
UI_SCALE_DEFAULT = 1;
gui_scale_idx = UI_SCALE_DEFAULT;
function ui_scale(idx) ( idx == 1 ? 1 : idx == 2 ? 1.25 : idx == 3 ? 1.5 : idx == 4 ? 2 : 0; );

// ---- layout grid ----
// Four columns of 234 on a 10 px margin and a 10 px gutter, used by the band
// panels, the bottom row and the curve panel alike.  COL(i) = 10 + i*244.
COL_W  = 234;
COL_DX = 244;
// The bottom row is three panels rather than four columns: four knobs do not
// fit a 234 column, so DYNAMICS is 322 wide and GAIN 390, which also gives the
// GR and RATIO meters a home beside the gains. The two outer panels still
// start on band 1's and band 4's grid lines.
DYN_W  = 322;
GAIN_W = 390;
BAND_Y = 326;             // band panel row
BOT_Y  = 496;             // crossover / dynamics / gain / display row

// Per-band tint, used for the lane labels and the four transfer curves so the
// two read as the same band.  Gain reduction stays orange and the ratio stays
// green everywhere, as in the single-band plugin - the tint identifies the
// band, never the quantity.
BTINT = fcb + 4;         // 4 * 3 floats, r,g,b per band
BTINT[0]  = 0.25; BTINT[1]  = 0.55; BTINT[2]  = 0.96;
BTINT[3]  = 0.20; BTINT[4]  = 0.78; BTINT[5]  = 0.90;
BTINT[6]  = 0.35; BTINT[7]  = 0.88; BTINT[8]  = 0.70;
BTINT[9]  = 0.65; BTINT[10] = 0.92; BTINT[11] = 0.45;

// @gfx only.  Knob on a LOG frequency scale: a linear knob spends 90% of its
// travel above 2 kHz on the top crossover, which makes the useful part of the
// control unusable.  The value field beside it stays linear and in plain Hz,
// so typing a number still does what it says.
function g_fknob(id, img, x, y, sidx, fmin, fmax, fdef) local(lv, nv)
(
  lv = log(slider(sidx));
  nv = gk_knob(id, img, x, y, 64, lv, log(fmin), log(fmax), log(fdef));
  nv != lv ? ( slider(sidx) = exp(nv); slider_automate(2^(sidx-1)); 1; ) : 0;
);

// @gfx only.  One band's curve: its threshold, the ratio it is holding right
// now, its band gain, and the global Output Gain on top.
function g_curve(thr, rsol, gdb, on)
  local(i, ld, gg, fac, ov, xx, yy)
(
  on ? gfx_set(gk_ac_r, gk_ac_g, gk_ac_b, 0.95) : gfx_set(0.34,0.35,0.38,0.8);
  i = 0;
  loop(121,
    ld = CURVE_LO + i*0.5;
    gg = (on && ld > thr) ? exp(-(ld - thr)*(1 - 1/max(rsol,1))*LN10_20) : 1;
    // plot what actually leaves the band: blend, then band gain and Output Gain
    fac = (1 - g_blend) + g_blend*gg;
    ov  = ld + log(max(fac, TINY))*DB20 + gdb + s_trim;
    xx  = g_qx + (ld - CURVE_LO)*g_qw/(-CURVE_LO);
    yy  = max(g_qy, min(g_qy+g_qh, g_qy + g_qh - (ov - CURVE_LO)*g_qh/g_span));
    i == 0 ? ( gfx_x = xx; gfx_y = yy; ) : gfx_lineto(xx, yy, 1);
    i += 1;
  );
);

@slider

update_params();

@block

// The UI thread only ever raises this flag; the derived values are rebuilt
// here, on the audio thread, so a widget change never enters update_params()
// underneath @sample.  See the threading note at the top of the file.
need_update ? ( need_update = 0; update_params(); );

// Display readouts, converted once per block rather than per sample.  @gfx
// reads these numbers and never calls lin2db itself.
gr_db  = -lin2db(mtr);
rt_now = rmtr;
bgrdb[0] = -lin2db(bmtr[0]);
bgrdb[1] = -lin2db(bmtr[1]);
bgrdb[2] = -lin2db(bmtr[2]);
bgrdb[3] = -lin2db(bmtr[3]);

@sample

// Input gain drives the entire plugin. The detectors, the curves and the dry
// side of the blend all see the boosted signal, so this pushes density, not
// level.
l = spl0 * ig;
r = spl1 * ig;

L.split_run(l);
R.split_run(r);

bnd1.band_run(L.b1, R.b1, ben[0], bthr[0], brelc[0], bclin[0]);
bnd2.band_run(L.b2, R.b2, ben[1], bthr[1], brelc[1], bclin[1]);
bnd3.band_run(L.b3, R.b3, ben[2], bthr[2], brelc[2], bclin[2]);
bnd4.band_run(L.b4, R.b4, ben[3], bthr[3], brelc[3], bclin[3]);

s_listen == 0 ? (
  ol  = bnd1.outL * bgain[0] + bnd2.outL * bgain[1]
      + bnd3.outL * bgain[2] + bnd4.outL * bgain[3];
  orr = bnd1.outR * bgain[0] + bnd2.outR * bgain[1]
      + bnd3.outR * bgain[2] + bnd4.outR * bgain[3];
) : s_listen == 1 ? ( ol = bnd1.outL * bgain[0]; orr = bnd1.outR * bgain[0]; )
  : s_listen == 2 ? ( ol = bnd2.outL * bgain[1]; orr = bnd2.outR * bgain[1]; )
  : s_listen == 3 ? ( ol = bnd3.outL * bgain[2]; orr = bnd3.outR * bgain[2]; )
  : ( ol = bnd4.outL * bgain[3]; orr = bnd4.outR * bgain[3]; );

// Output Gain, once, on the sum, after everything.
ol  *= trim;
orr *= trim;

spl0 = ol;
spl1 = orr;

// -------------------------------------------------------------- metering --

gmin = min(min(bnd1.gs, bnd2.gs), min(bnd3.gs, bnd4.gs));
rmax = max(max(bnd1.rs, bnd2.rs), max(bnd3.rs, bnd4.rs));
gmin < mtr  ? mtr  = gmin : mtr  = gmin + (mtr  - gmin) * 0.9995;
rmax > rmtr ? rmtr = rmax : rmtr = rmax + (rmtr - rmax) * 0.9995;

bnd1.gs < bmtr[0] ? bmtr[0] = bnd1.gs : bmtr[0] = bnd1.gs + (bmtr[0] - bnd1.gs) * 0.9995;
bnd2.gs < bmtr[1] ? bmtr[1] = bnd2.gs : bmtr[1] = bnd2.gs + (bmtr[1] - bnd2.gs) * 0.9995;
bnd3.gs < bmtr[2] ? bmtr[2] = bnd3.gs : bmtr[2] = bnd3.gs + (bmtr[2] - bnd3.gs) * 0.9995;
bnd4.gs < bmtr[3] ? bmtr[3] = bnd4.gs : bmtr[3] = bnd4.gs + (bmtr[3] - bnd4.gs) * 0.9995;
bnd1.rs > brmt[0] ? brmt[0] = bnd1.rs : brmt[0] = bnd1.rs + (brmt[0] - bnd1.rs) * 0.9995;
bnd2.rs > brmt[1] ? brmt[1] = bnd2.rs : brmt[1] = bnd2.rs + (brmt[1] - bnd2.rs) * 0.9995;
bnd3.rs > brmt[2] ? brmt[2] = bnd3.rs : brmt[2] = bnd3.rs + (brmt[2] - bnd3.rs) * 0.9995;
bnd4.rs > brmt[3] ? brmt[3] = bnd4.rs : brmt[3] = bnd4.rs + (brmt[3] - bnd4.rs) * 0.9995;

// ---- display history ----
// The summed pre-compression input is rebuilt from the delayed band inputs, so
// it lines up sample for sample with the output and the scope shows cause and
// effect on the same column.  Converted to dB here, on the audio thread, so
// @gfx only ever reads numbers.
dinL = bnd1.inL + bnd2.inL + bnd3.inL + bnd4.inL;
dinR = bnd1.inR + bnd2.inR + bnd3.inR + bnd4.inR;

acc_in  = max(acc_in,  max(abs(dinL), abs(dinR)));
acc_out = max(acc_out, max(abs(ol),   abs(orr)));

bnd1.gs < accG[0] ? accG[0] = bnd1.gs;
bnd2.gs < accG[1] ? accG[1] = bnd2.gs;
bnd3.gs < accG[2] ? accG[2] = bnd3.gs;
bnd4.gs < accG[3] ? accG[3] = bnd4.gs;
bnd1.rs > accR[0] ? accR[0] = bnd1.rs;
bnd2.rs > accR[1] ? accR[1] = bnd2.rs;
bnd3.rs > accR[2] ? accR[2] = bnd3.rs;
bnd4.rs > accR[3] ? accR[3] = bnd4.rs;

acc_n += 1;
acc_n >= col_len ? (
  HIST_IN[hpos]  = lin2db(acc_in);
  HIST_OUT[hpos] = lin2db(acc_out);
  HIST_GR[hpos]                = -lin2db(accG[0]);   // positive dB of reduction
  HIST_GR[HIST_N + hpos]       = -lin2db(accG[1]);
  HIST_GR[HIST_N * 2 + hpos]   = -lin2db(accG[2]);
  HIST_GR[HIST_N * 3 + hpos]   = -lin2db(accG[3]);
  HIST_RT[hpos]                = accR[0];
  HIST_RT[HIST_N + hpos]       = accR[1];
  HIST_RT[HIST_N * 2 + hpos]   = accR[2];
  HIST_RT[HIST_N * 3 + hpos]   = accR[3];
  hpos = (hpos + 1) & HMASK;
  acc_in = 0; acc_out = 0; acc_n = 0;
  memset(accG, 1, 4); memset(accR, 1, 4);
);

@gfx 986 668

// Reminder: every local here is g_-prefixed, nothing calls a function the
// audio thread also calls, and the transfer-curve maths is a deliberate copy
// of the @sample curve.  See the threading note at the top of the file.
// EEL2 is case-insensitive, so a bare w, n, a, g, l, r, f1 or box here would
// silently overwrite an audio-thread global mid-sample.

gk_begin();
g_chg = 0;

// Take the scale from the shared global, or seed it if nothing has yet.
gmem[GM_UI_SCALE] > 0 ? gui_scale_idx = gmem[GM_UI_SCALE] - 1
                      : gmem[GM_UI_SCALE] = gui_scale_idx + 1;

// gk_begin set gk_S from gfx_ext_retina (1 or 2). Fixed percentages are taken
// relative to that, so 100% stays pixel-exact on a HiDPI display; Fit divides
// the physical window by the design size, which already includes it.
g_ret = gk_S;
g_scl = ui_scale(gui_scale_idx);
gk_S = max(0.5, g_scl > 0 ? g_ret * g_scl : min(gfx_w / UI_W, gfx_h / UI_H));
gfx_setfont(1, gk_font, 11*gk_S);
gfx_setfont(2, gk_font, 13*gk_S, 'b');
gfx_setfont(1);

// Display values, derived HERE from the raw slider variables rather than read
// from the audio thread's copies.  This matters: slider_automate() does not
// re-run @slider, so a widget change reaches update_params() only through the
// need_update flag that @block picks up - and @BLOCK DOES NOT RUN ON A STOPPED
// TRACK.  Reading ceil_db / blend / f1..f3 here would leave the ceiling line,
// the band headers and the whole curve panel frozen at their old values while
// the knob visibly moved, until the user pressed play.  These lines are a
// deliberate duplicate of the corresponding lines in update_params(); keep the
// two in step.  The audio thread is unaffected - @block always runs before the
// @sample calls it precedes, so the DSP never processes a sample with stale
// coefficients.
g_ceil  = s_ceil;
g_blend = s_blend * 0.01;
g_f1 = s_x1;
g_f2 = max(s_x2, g_f1 * 1.1);
g_f3 = max(s_x3, g_f2 * 1.1);
g_f3 = min(g_f3, srate * 0.45);
g_f2 = min(g_f2, g_f3 / 1.1);
g_f1 = min(g_f1, g_f2 / 1.1);

// Per-band ceilings, from the same tilt line update_params() builds: s_tilt dB
// per octave across log frequency, sampled at each band's geometric centre,
// pivoted on the geometric mean of those centres so the Ceiling knob stays the
// average of the four. Another deliberate duplicate - keep it in step with
// update_params(), and note the thresholds are clamped under their own band's
// ceiling here too, or a tilted curve would bend at the wrong place.
g_fc1 = sqrt(FC_LO * g_f1);
g_fc2 = sqrt(g_f1 * g_f2);
g_fc3 = sqrt(g_f2 * g_f3);
g_fc4 = sqrt(g_f3 * min(FC_HI, srate * 0.5));
g_fpiv = exp((log(g_fc1) + log(g_fc2) + log(g_fc3) + log(g_fc4)) * 0.25);
g_bc1 = g_ceil + s_tilt * log(g_fc1/g_fpiv)/log(2);
g_bc2 = g_ceil + s_tilt * log(g_fc2/g_fpiv)/log(2);
g_bc3 = g_ceil + s_tilt * log(g_fc3/g_fpiv)/log(2);
g_bc4 = g_ceil + s_tilt * log(g_fc4/g_fpiv)/log(2);

gk_header(0, 10, 10, 966, 22, "VARIABLE RATIO COMPRESSOR MB", -1, 0);

// Scale selector and help button, far right of the header. Their x is pinned
// to the right edge of what is actually on screen, so a scale too large for
// the window can never push these controls out of reach.
g_rr = min(UI_W - 20, gfx_w/gk_S - 10);
g_dx = g_rr - 86;
g_i = gk_dropdown(40, g_dx, 13, 64, 16, gui_scale_idx, "Fit|100%|125%|150%|200%",
      gui_scale_idx == 0 ? "Fit"  : gui_scale_idx == 1 ? "100%" :
      gui_scale_idx == 2 ? "125%" : gui_scale_idx == 3 ? "150%" : "200%");
g_i != gui_scale_idx ? ( gui_scale_idx = g_i; gmem[GM_UI_SCALE] = g_i + 1; );

gk_button(41, g_rr - 18, 13, 18, 16, help_open, "?") ? (
  help_open = !help_open;
  help_open && !help_lines ? help_lines = gk_help_load(HELP_FILE, HELP_BASE, HELP_MAX);
);

// Freeze the controls underneath while the help is open, keeping the two
// header controls live so the help can be closed again.
help_open ? gk_help_input(40, 41);

// ================= summed scrolling display =================
// Plotted in dB, FLOORDB at the centre line and 0 dB at the edges, exactly as
// the single-band plugin plots it. The gain-reduction overlay and the ratio
// trace here are the AGGREGATE across the four bands - deepest reduction,
// highest ratio - which is what the GR and RATIO meters show. The per-band
// detail is in the lanes underneath.
accent(0.149, 0.784, 0.902);   // cyan
gk_nine(gk_img_wave_frame, 16, 8, 10, 40, 722, 150);
gk_wave_geom(10, 40, 722, 150);
g_ix = gk_wv_ix; g_iy = gk_wv_iy; g_iw = gk_wv_iw; g_ih = gk_wv_ih;
g_cy = gk_wv_cy; g_half = gk_wv_half;
g_yscale = g_half/(-FLOORDB);          // device px per dB, from the centre out

// One pass, and each drawn column takes the MAX over every history slot it
// covers. 1024 slots into ~700 pixels means point-sampling would silently
// drop transients, which made the display disagree with the ear.
g_iwi = floor(g_iw);
g_i = 0;
g_have = 0;
loop(g_iwi,
  g_a = floor(g_i*HIST_N/g_iwi);
  g_b2 = floor((g_i+1)*HIST_N/g_iwi);
  g_b2 <= g_a ? ( g_b2 = g_a + 1; );

  g_din = FLOORDB - 200; g_dout = FLOORDB - 200; g_dgr = 0; g_drt = 1;
  g_k = g_a;
  while (g_k < g_b2) (
    g_p = (hpos + g_k) & HMASK;
    g_din  = max(g_din,  HIST_IN[g_p]);
    g_dout = max(g_dout, HIST_OUT[g_p]);
    g_dgr  = max(g_dgr, max(max(HIST_GR[g_p], HIST_GR[HIST_N + g_p]),
                            max(HIST_GR[HIST_N*2 + g_p], HIST_GR[HIST_N*3 + g_p])));
    g_drt  = max(g_drt, max(max(HIST_RT[g_p], HIST_RT[HIST_N + g_p]),
                            max(HIST_RT[HIST_N*2 + g_p], HIST_RT[HIST_N*3 + g_p])));
    g_k += 1;
  );

  g_hin  = min(g_half, max(0, (g_din  - FLOORDB)*g_yscale));
  g_hout = min(g_half, max(0, (g_dout - FLOORDB)*g_yscale));
  g_x = g_ix + g_i;
  // older columns fade out, the same gesture gk_wave_draw makes
  g_fade = 0.4 + 0.6*g_i/g_iwi;
  // draw the shorter trace last so it stays visible inside the taller one.
  // The input is the translucent one: where the two overlap it tints the
  // output rather than hiding it, so the grey standing outside the cyan - the
  // gain reduction - is the thing the eye lands on.
  g_hout >= g_hin ? (
    g_hout > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.20,0.78,0.90,0.90*g_fade); gfx_line(g_x, g_cy-g_hout, g_x, g_cy+g_hout); );
    g_hin  > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.45,0.52,0.62,0.55*g_fade); gfx_line(g_x, g_cy-g_hin,  g_x, g_cy+g_hin);  );
  ) : (
    g_hin  > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.45,0.52,0.62,0.55*g_fade); gfx_line(g_x, g_cy-g_hin,  g_x, g_cy+g_hin);  );
    g_hout > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.20,0.78,0.90,0.90*g_fade); gfx_line(g_x, g_cy-g_hout, g_x, g_cy+g_hout); );
  );

  // Gain reduction, hanging from the top edge: translucent fill with a solid
  // bottom edge, so it reads over the waveform rather than hiding it. The
  // scale is FIXED at 0..GR_FULL_DB and does not follow any slider.
  g_hh = min(g_dgr/GR_FULL_DB, 1)*g_ih;
  g_hh > 0 ? (
    gfx_set(0.941,0.588,0.157,0.22*g_fade);
    gfx_line(g_x, g_iy, g_x, g_iy+g_hh);
    gfx_set(0.980,0.640,0.200,0.95*g_fade);
    gfx_rect(g_x, g_iy+g_hh, 1, max(1, gk_S));
  );

  // Ratio, as a line rising from the bottom edge on a fixed log scale. A line
  // rather than a fill: two filled overlays on one plot and neither is
  // readable.
  g_ry = g_iy + g_ih - min(log(max(g_drt,1))/LN_RATIO, 1)*g_ih;
  gfx_set(0.42,0.95,0.62,0.85*g_fade);
  g_have ? gfx_line(g_x, g_ry, g_px, g_py);
  g_px = g_x; g_py = g_ry; g_have = 1;

  g_i += 1;
);

// centre line, then the ceiling mirrored about it.  There is no threshold line
// on this plot: there are four thresholds and they live on the bands' own
// curves in the CURVE panel.
gfx_set(0.30,0.30,0.34,1); gfx_line(g_ix, g_cy, g_ix+g_iw, g_cy);

g_hc = min(g_half, max(0,(g_ceil - FLOORDB)*g_yscale));
gfx_set(0.941,0.784,0.235,0.9);
gfx_line(g_ix, g_cy-g_hc, g_ix+g_iw, g_cy-g_hc);
gfx_line(g_ix, g_cy+g_hc, g_ix+g_iw, g_cy+g_hc);

// "now" edge, from the kit, so this display matches the other Magnolius plots
gfx_getimgdim(gk_img_playhead_line, g_sw, g_sh);
g_sw > 0 ? gfx_blit(gk_img_playhead_line,1,0, 0,0,g_sw,g_sh, g_ix+g_iw-3*gk_S, g_iy, 6*gk_S, g_ih);
gfx_getimgdim(gk_img_playhead_marker, g_sw, g_sh);
g_sw > 0 ? gfx_blit(gk_img_playhead_marker,1,0, 0,0,g_sw,g_sh, g_ix+g_iw-7*gk_S, g_iy+g_ih-6*gk_S, 14*gk_S, 10*gk_S);

// Legend, inside the frame: each word in the colour of the thing it names,
// which is the only thing that says which trace is which. Placed as the
// single-band plugin places its own - absolute logical coordinates off the
// window, font 1, y = 45, first word at x = 18 - so the two read the same in
// a chain. It gets a backdrop because the gain-reduction overlay hangs from
// the TOP and prints straight through the words whenever the compressor is
// working hard, which is exactly when someone is reading them.
gfx_setfont(1);
gfx_set(0.04,0.045,0.05,0.72);
gfx_rect(14*gk_S, 43*gk_S, 150*gk_S, 15*gk_S);
gfx_set(0.45,0.52,0.62,1);    gfx_x =  18*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("IN");
gfx_set(0.20,0.78,0.90,1);    gfx_x =  38*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("OUT");
gfx_set(0.980,0.640,0.200,1); gfx_x =  68*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("GR");
gfx_set(0.42,0.95,0.62,1);    gfx_x =  91*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("RATIO");
gfx_set(0.941,0.784,0.235,1); gfx_x = 133*gk_S; gfx_y = 45*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("CEIL");

// ================= per-band lanes =================
// Four slim lanes on the same time axis as the plot above: each band's gain
// reduction hanging from its lane top (orange, 0..GR_FULL_DB) and its solved
// ratio rising from the lane bottom (green, 1..RATIO_FULL on a log scale).
// A disabled band draws grey, so "off" is visible without reading the header.
gk_nine(gk_img_wave_frame, 16, 8, 10, 196, 722, 120);
gk_wave_geom(10, 196, 722, 120);
g_lx = gk_wv_ix; g_ly = gk_wv_iy; g_lw = gk_wv_iw; g_lh = gk_wv_ih;
g_lane = g_lh/4;
g_lwi = floor(g_lw);

g_b = 0;
loop(4,
  g_ly0 = g_ly + g_b*g_lane;
  g_on  = slider(14 + g_b*5);
  g_bas = g_b*HIST_N;

  g_b > 0 ? ( gfx_set(0.19,0.19,0.22,1); gfx_line(g_lx, g_ly0, g_lx+g_lw, g_ly0); );

  g_have = 0;
  g_i = 0;
  loop(g_lwi,
    g_a = floor(g_i*HIST_N/g_lwi);
    g_b2 = floor((g_i+1)*HIST_N/g_lwi);
    g_b2 <= g_a ? ( g_b2 = g_a + 1; );

    g_dgr = 0; g_drt = 1;
    g_k = g_a;
    while (g_k < g_b2) (
      g_p = (hpos + g_k) & HMASK;
      g_dgr = max(g_dgr, HIST_GR[g_bas + g_p]);
      g_drt = max(g_drt, HIST_RT[g_bas + g_p]);
      g_k += 1;
    );

    g_x = g_lx + g_i;
    g_fade = 0.4 + 0.6*g_i/g_lwi;

    g_hh = min(g_dgr/GR_FULL_DB, 1)*(g_lane - 2);
    (g_on && g_hh > 0) ? (
      gfx_set(0.941,0.588,0.157,0.22*g_fade);
      gfx_line(g_x, g_ly0+1, g_x, g_ly0+1+g_hh);
      gfx_set(0.980,0.640,0.200,0.95*g_fade);
      gfx_rect(g_x, g_ly0+1+g_hh, 1, max(1, gk_S));
    );

    g_ry = g_ly0 + g_lane - 1 - min(log(max(g_drt,1))/LN_RATIO, 1)*(g_lane - 2);
    g_on ? gfx_set(0.42,0.95,0.62,0.85*g_fade) : gfx_set(0.26,0.28,0.27,0.85);
    g_have ? gfx_line(g_x, g_ry, g_px, g_py);
    g_px = g_x; g_py = g_ry; g_have = 1;

    g_i += 1;
  );

  // lane label, in the band's tint so the lane and its curve agree
  g_on ? gfx_set(BTINT[g_b*3], BTINT[g_b*3+1], BTINT[g_b*3+2], 1)
       : gfx_set(gk_dim_r, gk_dim_g, gk_dim_b, 1);
  gfx_x = g_lx + 4*gk_S; gfx_y = g_ly0 + 2*gk_S;
  gfx_drawstr("B"); gfx_drawnumber(g_b + 1, 0);
  g_on ? 0 : gfx_drawstr("  off");

  g_b += 1;
);

// ================= transfer curves =================
// One curve per band, in the band's tint, each drawn from the LIVE solved
// ratio of that band - so they bend as the plugin works, which is the whole
// point of a compressor that derives its own ratio. The maths is deliberately
// a copy of the @sample curve: @gfx must not share a function with the audio
// thread. Keep the two in step.
gk_panel(742, 40, 234, 276);
gk_header(0, 742, 40, 234, 22, "CURVE", -1, 0);
g_qx = 750*gk_S; g_qy = 70*gk_S; g_qw = 218*gk_S; g_qh = 220*gk_S;
g_span = CURVE_HI - CURVE_LO;                 // dB spanned vertically

gfx_set(0.08,0.085,0.095,1); gfx_rect(g_qx,g_qy,g_qw,g_qh);
gfx_set(0.20,0.20,0.23,1);
g_i = 0; loop(5,
  gfx_line(g_qx, g_qy+g_qh*g_i*0.25, g_qx+g_qw, g_qy+g_qh*g_i*0.25);
  gfx_line(g_qx+g_qw*g_i*0.25, g_qy, g_qx+g_qw*g_i*0.25, g_qy+g_qh);
  g_i += 1;
);

// unity reference: what the input would do untouched
gfx_set(0.40,0.40,0.44,1);
gfx_line(g_qx, g_qy+g_qh, g_qx+g_qw, g_qy+g_qh - (-CURVE_LO)*g_qh/g_span);

// Ceiling, as a horizontal marker on the output axis. It is the per-band
// target, so Output Gain is not folded into it - four bands each held here
// still sum above it.
//
// At Tilt 0 the four bands share one ceiling and it draws as one yellow dashed
// line, exactly as it always has. Tilted, each band gets its own line in its
// own tint at its own height, and the spread between them IS the tilt: that is
// the whole picture of what the control does. Hidden curves are left out, so
// the panel does not fill up with lines for traces that are not there.
s_tilt == 0 ? (
  g_cl = g_qy + g_qh - (g_ceil - CURVE_LO)*g_qh/g_span;
  gfx_set(0.941,0.784,0.235,0.65);
  g_i = 0; loop(floor(g_qw/8),
    gfx_line(g_qx+g_i*8, g_cl, g_qx+g_i*8+4, g_cl);
    g_i += 1;
  );
) : (
  g_b = 0;
  loop(4,
    (curve_mask & 2^g_b) ? (
      g_cl = g_qy + g_qh -
             ((g_b == 0 ? g_bc1 : g_b == 1 ? g_bc2 : g_b == 2 ? g_bc3 : g_bc4)
              - CURVE_LO)*g_qh/g_span;
      g_cl = max(g_qy, min(g_qy + g_qh, g_cl));
      gfx_set(BTINT[g_b*3], BTINT[g_b*3+1], BTINT[g_b*3+2], 0.55);
      g_i = 0; loop(floor(g_qw/8),
        gfx_line(g_qx+g_i*8, g_cl, g_qx+g_i*8+4, g_cl);
        g_i += 1;
      );
    );
    g_b += 1;
  );
);

// Legend, and the only control for which curves are drawn: clicking B1..B4
// toggles that band's curve. Four curves on one plot is the readable limit,
// and comparing two of them means being able to put the other two away.
// Hit-tested BEFORE the curves are drawn so a click takes effect on the frame
// it happens, and it uses the same gk_clicked / gk_hit pattern as the kit's
// own widgets, so the help overlay suppresses it along with everything else.
// The state is GUI-only, so it is serialised rather than made a slider.
gfx_setfont(1);
g_b = 0;
loop(4,
  g_lgx = 752 + g_b*26;
  g_bit = 2^g_b;
  gk_clicked && gk_hit(g_lgx - 3, 294, 24, 15) ? (
    curve_mask = (curve_mask & g_bit) ? curve_mask - g_bit : curve_mask + g_bit;
  );
  g_vis = (curve_mask & g_bit) > 0;

  // three states, in descending prominence: shown on a live band, shown on a
  // disabled band (grey, matching the grey curve), hidden.
  g_vis ? (
    slider(14 + g_b*5) ? gfx_set(BTINT[g_b*3], BTINT[g_b*3+1], BTINT[g_b*3+2], 1)
                       : gfx_set(gk_dim_r, gk_dim_g, gk_dim_b, 1);
  ) : gfx_set(gk_dim_r, gk_dim_g, gk_dim_b, 0.45);
  gfx_x = g_lgx*gk_S; gfx_y = 296*gk_S;
  gfx_drawstr("B"); gfx_drawnumber(g_b + 1, 0);
  // underline in the band's tint while the curve is shown - without it
  // "greyed out" is the only cue that the label is a switch at all
  g_vis ? (
    gfx_set(BTINT[g_b*3], BTINT[g_b*3+1], BTINT[g_b*3+2], 0.8);
    gfx_rect(g_lgx*gk_S, 308*gk_S, 15*gk_S, max(1, gk_S));
  );
  g_b += 1;
);

(curve_mask & 1) ? ( accent(BTINT[0], BTINT[1],  BTINT[2]);  g_curve(min(s_th1, g_bc1 - 0.1), bnd1.rs, s_gn1, s_en1); );
(curve_mask & 2) ? ( accent(BTINT[3], BTINT[4],  BTINT[5]);  g_curve(min(s_th2, g_bc2 - 0.1), bnd2.rs, s_gn2, s_en2); );
(curve_mask & 4) ? ( accent(BTINT[6], BTINT[7],  BTINT[8]);  g_curve(min(s_th3, g_bc3 - 0.1), bnd3.rs, s_gn3, s_en3); );
(curve_mask & 8) ? ( accent(BTINT[9], BTINT[10], BTINT[11]); g_curve(min(s_th4, g_bc4 - 0.1), bnd4.rs, s_gn4, s_en4); );
gfx_set(gk_dim_r, gk_dim_g, gk_dim_b, 1);
gfx_x = 894*gk_S; gfx_y = 296*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("dB in / out");

// ================= band panels =================
// One panel per band, all four on the same grid: the LED in the header is the
// band's Enable, SOLO is the Listen control, and the bar beside it is that
// band's live gain reduction on the same 0..GR_FULL_DB scale as everything
// else. Sliders run 14+5b (enable), +1 threshold, +2 release, +3 gain.
//
// Alt on any of the three per-band controls moves ALL FOUR bands together -
// see the block after the loop. The loop only records which control moved and
// by how much, because the group move needs every band's value from BEFORE
// this frame and the bands after this one have not been drawn yet.
g_gk = 0;

g_b = 0;
loop(4,
  g_bx = 10 + g_b*COL_DX;
  g_si = 14 + g_b*5;
  g_on = slider(g_si);

  // header title carries the band's frequency range, from the ordered
  // crossovers - which is the only place the ordering clamp is visible
  g_b == 0 ? sprintf(#ttl, "BAND 1         to %.0f Hz", g_f1) :
  g_b == 1 ? sprintf(#ttl, "BAND 2   %.0f - %.0f Hz", g_f1, g_f2) :
  g_b == 2 ? sprintf(#ttl, "BAND 3   %.0f - %.0f Hz", g_f2, g_f3) :
             sprintf(#ttl, "BAND 4      %.0f Hz up", g_f3);

  gk_panel(g_bx, BAND_Y, COL_W, 160);
  g_v = gk_header(0, g_bx, BAND_Y, COL_W, 22, #ttl, IMG_LED, g_on);
  g_v != g_on ? (
    slider(g_si) = g_v; slider_automate(2^(g_si-1));
    g_on = g_v; g_chg = 1;
  );

  // Cyan for the two dynamics controls, yellow for the gain. The band panels
  // keep cyan while the global CROSSOVER and DYNAMICS panels are orange, so
  // the per-band controls stay visually separate from the ones that apply to
  // all four at once.
  g_on ? accent(0.149, 0.784, 0.902) : accent(0.42, 0.44, 0.47);

  g_t0 = slider(g_si + 1); g_r0 = slider(g_si + 2); g_n0 = slider(g_si + 3);

  g_ct = gk_knob_slider(10 + g_b*3, 0, g_bx +  10, BAND_Y + 30, 64, g_si + 1, -60, 0, -18);
  g_cr = gk_knob_slider(11 + g_b*3, 0, g_bx +  85, BAND_Y + 30, 64, g_si + 2, 10, 2000,
                        g_b == 0 ? 400 : g_b == 1 ? 250 : g_b == 2 ? 150 : 80);
  g_cn = gk_knob_slider(12 + g_b*3, 2, g_bx + 160, BAND_Y + 30, 64, g_si + 3, -20, 20, 0);

  gk_text_c(g_bx +  42, BAND_Y + 98, "Thresh", 1);
  gk_text_c(g_bx + 117, BAND_Y + 98, "Release", 1);
  gk_text_c(g_bx + 192, BAND_Y + 98, "Gain", 1);

  sprintf(#s, "%.1f dB", slider(g_si + 1));
  g_ct |= gk_field_edit_slider(50 + g_b*3, g_bx +  12, BAND_Y + 112, 60, 16, g_si + 1, -60, 0, -18, #s);
  sprintf(#s, "%.0f ms", slider(g_si + 2));
  g_cr |= gk_field_edit_slider(51 + g_b*3, g_bx +  87, BAND_Y + 112, 60, 16, g_si + 2, 10, 2000,
                               g_b == 0 ? 400 : g_b == 1 ? 250 : g_b == 2 ? 150 : 80, #s);
  sprintf(#s, "%+.1f dB", slider(g_si + 3));
  g_cn |= gk_field_edit_slider(52 + g_b*3, g_bx + 162, BAND_Y + 112, 60, 16, g_si + 3, -20, 20, 0, #s);

  g_chg |= g_ct | g_cr | g_cn;

  // Alt held: remember what moved, what it was before, and by how much.
  // Threshold and Gain carry a difference in dB; Release carries a RATIO,
  // because what relates 400 ms to 80 ms is a factor, not a number of
  // milliseconds - adding the same ms to both would collapse the fast band
  // into its floor while the slow one barely moved.
  (mouse_cap & 16) && !(mouse_cap & 4) ? (
    g_ct ? ( g_gk = 1; g_gb = g_b; g_gbase = g_t0; g_gd = slider(g_si+1) - g_t0; ) :
    g_cr ? ( g_gk = 2; g_gb = g_b; g_gbase = g_r0; g_gd = slider(g_si+2) / g_r0; ) :
    g_cn ? ( g_gk = 3; g_gb = g_b; g_gbase = g_n0; g_gd = slider(g_si+3) - g_n0; );
  );

  // SOLO writes the Listen slider, so it stays one automatable parameter:
  // clicking a soloed band returns to All bands.
  gk_button(42 + g_b, g_bx + 10, BAND_Y + 134, 44, 16, s_listen == g_b + 1, "SOLO") ? (
    s_listen = s_listen == g_b + 1 ? 0 : g_b + 1;
    slider_automate(2^11);
  );

  // live gain reduction for this band, growing to the right
  g_mx = (g_bx + 62)*gk_S; g_my = (BAND_Y + 134)*gk_S;
  g_mw = 162*gk_S; g_mh = 16*gk_S;
  gfx_set(0.08,0.085,0.095,1); gfx_rect(g_mx, g_my, g_mw, g_mh);
  g_gv = g_on ? bgrdb[g_b] : 0;
  g_bw = min(g_gv/GR_FULL_DB, 1)*g_mw;
  g_bw > 0 ? (
    gfx_set(0.980,0.640,0.200,0.80); gfx_rect(g_mx, g_my, g_bw, g_mh);
    gfx_set(1.0,0.78,0.35,1); gfx_rect(g_mx + g_bw - max(1,gk_S), g_my, max(1,gk_S), g_mh);
  );
  gfx_set(0.28,0.28,0.32,1); gfx_rect(g_mx, g_my, g_mw, g_mh, 0);
  gfx_set(gk_dim_r, gk_dim_g, gk_dim_b, 1);
  gfx_x = g_mx + 5*gk_S; gfx_y = g_my + 2*gk_S; gfx_drawstr("GR");
  g_on ? sprintf(#s, "%.1f dB", g_gv) : sprintf(#s, "off");
  gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
  gfx_set(gk_fg_r, gk_fg_g, gk_fg_b, 1);
  gfx_x = g_mx + g_mw - 5*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my + 2*gk_S; gfx_drawstr(#s);

  g_b += 1;
);

// ---- alt on a band control: move every band by the same amount ----
// The point is to keep a balance you have already dialled in. The four bands
// move as ONE rigid body: every band starts from the value it had before this
// frame and takes the same step, so the offsets between them come out
// untouched. That also means the group stops when the FIRST band reaches an
// end of its range, including the one under the mouse - the knob you are
// dragging goes still rather than letting the others fall out of formation.
//
// Threshold and Gain step by a difference in dB. Release scales by a ratio,
// so the bands keep their relationship to each other; on an additive step the
// 80 ms band would hit its 10 ms floor after a 70 ms drag and take the whole
// group with it.
g_gk > 0 ? (
  g_off = g_gk;                                     // 1 thresh, 2 rel, 3 gain
  g_lo = g_gk == 1 ? -60 : g_gk == 2 ? 10   : -20;
  g_hi = g_gk == 1 ?   0 : g_gk == 2 ? 2000 :  20;

  // how far the group may move before any band leaves its range
  g_gk == 2 ? ( g_pmax = g_hi/g_lo;  g_pmin = g_lo/g_hi;  )
            : ( g_pmax = g_hi-g_lo;  g_pmin = g_lo-g_hi;  );
  g_b = 0;
  loop(4,
    g_v = g_b == g_gb ? g_gbase : slider(14 + g_b*5 + g_off);
    g_gk == 2 ? ( g_pmax = min(g_pmax, g_hi/g_v);  g_pmin = max(g_pmin, g_lo/g_v);  )
              : ( g_pmax = min(g_pmax, g_hi-g_v);  g_pmin = max(g_pmin, g_lo-g_v);  );
    g_b += 1;
  );
  g_gm = max(g_pmin, min(g_pmax, g_gd));

  // one automate call for the four sliders, so the host sees one gesture
  g_mask = 0;
  g_b = 0;
  loop(4,
    g_sj = 14 + g_b*5 + g_off;
    g_v = g_b == g_gb ? g_gbase : slider(g_sj);
    slider(g_sj) = g_gk == 2 ? g_v * g_gm : g_v + g_gm;
    g_mask += 2^(g_sj - 1);
    g_b += 1;
  );
  slider_automate(g_mask);
  g_chg = 1;
);

// ================= crossover =================
// Log-scaled knobs, plain-Hz fields. The three frequencies are kept ordered
// and inside Nyquist by update_params(), so a knob pushed past its neighbour
// stops having an effect rather than scrambling the tree - the band headers
// show what the DSP actually used.
gk_panel(10, BOT_Y, COL_W, 138);
gk_header(0, 10, BOT_Y, COL_W, 22, "CROSSOVER", -1, 0);
accent(0.941, 0.588, 0.157);   // orange

g_chg |= g_fknob(22, IMG_KNOB_ORANGE,  20, BOT_Y + 30, 2, 20, 500, 120);
g_chg |= g_fknob(23, IMG_KNOB_ORANGE,  95, BOT_Y + 30, 3, 200, 3000, 800);
g_chg |= g_fknob(24, IMG_KNOB_ORANGE, 170, BOT_Y + 30, 4, 1500, 16000, 5000);
gk_text_c( 52, BOT_Y + 98, "Low", 1);
gk_text_c(127, BOT_Y + 98, "Mid", 1);
gk_text_c(202, BOT_Y + 98, "High", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%.0f Hz", s_x1);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(62,  22, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 2, 20, 500, 120, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.0f Hz", s_x2);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(63,  97, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 3, 200, 3000, 800, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.0f Hz", s_x3);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(64, 172, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 4, 1500, 16000, 5000, #s);

// ================= dynamics =================
// The controls every band shares. Ceiling is per band and shared by all four,
// which is why it is here and not on the band panels - and Tilt is what turns
// that one number into four. This panel is 322 wide rather than a 234 column:
// four knobs do not fit one, and these four are the plugin's core.
gk_panel(254, BOT_Y, DYN_W, 138);
gk_header(0, 254, BOT_Y, DYN_W, 22, "DYNAMICS", -1, 0);
accent(0.941, 0.588, 0.157);   // orange

g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(25, IMG_KNOB_ORANGE,    264, BOT_Y + 30, 64,  5, -36, 0, -6);
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(32, IMG_KNOB_ORANGE_BI, 343, BOT_Y + 30, 64, 18, -6, 6, 0);
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(26, IMG_KNOB_ORANGE,    422, BOT_Y + 30, 64, 11, 1, 10, 3);
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(27, IMG_KNOB_ORANGE,    501, BOT_Y + 30, 64, 10, 0.5, 20, 5);
gk_text_c(296, BOT_Y + 98, "Ceiling", 1);
gk_text_c(375, BOT_Y + 98, "Tilt", 1);
gk_text_c(454, BOT_Y + 98, "Max Ratio", 1);
gk_text_c(533, BOT_Y + 98, "Lookahead", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%.2f dB", s_ceil);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(65, 266, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16,  5, -36, 0, -6, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%+.1f /oct", s_tilt);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(71, 345, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 18, -6, 6, 0, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f:1", s_maxr);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(66, 424, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 11, 1, 10, 3, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f ms", s_la);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(67, 503, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 10, 0.5, 20, 5, #s);

// ================= gain =================
// Signal order, left to right: drive in, how much of the compressed signal to
// keep, level out. Input Gain is a genuine pre-compressor drive - the
// detectors, the curves and the dry half of the blend all see it - while
// Output Gain is a plain trim on the sum.
gk_panel(586, BOT_Y, GAIN_W, 138);
gk_header(0, 586, BOT_Y, GAIN_W, 22, "GAIN", -1, 0);
accent(0.941, 0.784, 0.235);   // yellow

// Alt-drag links the two gains in opposite directions - see the block after
// the fields. Both values are captured before any widget can move them.
g_in0 = s_in; g_out0 = s_trim;

g_cin   = gk_knob_slider(28, 1, 596, BOT_Y + 30, 64, 1, -12, 24, 0);
g_chg |= gk_knob_slider(29, 1, 671, BOT_Y + 30, 64, 6, 0, 100, 100);
g_cout  = gk_knob_slider(30, 2, 746, BOT_Y + 30, 64, 7, -20, 20, 0);
gk_text_c(628, BOT_Y + 98, "Input Gain", 1);
gk_text_c(703, BOT_Y + 98, "Blend", 1);
gk_text_c(778, BOT_Y + 98, "Output Gain", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%+.1f dB", s_in);
g_cin  |= gk_field_edit_slider(68, 598, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 1, -12, 24, 0, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.0f %%", s_blend);
g_chg |= gk_field_edit_slider(69, 673, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 6, 0, 100, 100, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%+.1f dB", s_trim);
g_cout |= gk_field_edit_slider(70, 748, BOT_Y + 112, 60, 16, 7, -20, 20, 0, #s);

// Alt-drag on either gain moves the other one the opposite way by the same
// number of dB: drive harder into the bands and take the same amount back out,
// so what changes is how hard the plugin works and not the level. It reads the
// delta the widgets just produced rather than duplicating their drag maths, so
// it covers the knob, the wheel and the value field alike - and it is gated on
// those widgets' own change flags, so an automation write landing mid-frame
// cannot be mistaken for a drag. Ctrl is excluded
// because a reset is a jump to a default, not a gesture with a delta worth
// mirroring. Each value still clamps to its own range, so the link goes
// one-sided once either end runs out of travel.
g_chg |= g_cin | g_cout;
(mouse_cap & 16) && !(mouse_cap & 4) ? (
  g_cin ? (
    s_trim = gk_clamp(g_out0 - (s_in - g_in0), -20, 20);
    slider_automate(2^6);
  ) : g_cout ? (
    s_in = gk_clamp(g_in0 - (s_trim - g_out0), -12, 24);
    slider_automate(2^0);
  );
);

// ---- meters, in the right-hand third of the GAIN panel ----
// The aggregate of the four bands - deepest gain reduction, highest ratio -
// matching the overlays on the summed plot above. They sit with the gains
// because that is where you look while setting level. Live data, so both are
// drawn with primitives rather than blitted.
g_my = (BOT_Y + 30)*gk_S; g_mh = 64*gk_S;
g_g1 = 856*gk_S; g_g2 = 916*gk_S; g_mw = 40*gk_S;

gfx_set(0.08,0.085,0.095,1);
gfx_rect(g_g1, g_my, g_mw, g_mh);
gfx_rect(g_g2, g_my, g_mw, g_mh);

gfx_set(0.22,0.22,0.25,1);
g_i = 1; loop(3,
  gfx_line(g_g1, g_my+g_mh*g_i*0.25, g_g1+g_mw, g_my+g_mh*g_i*0.25);
  gfx_line(g_g2, g_my+g_mh*g_i*0.25, g_g2+g_mw, g_my+g_mh*g_i*0.25);
  g_i += 1;
);

// gain reduction: grows DOWNWARD from the top, the same direction as the
// overlays on the plot and in the lanes, so the three read as one gesture
g_bh = min(gr_db/GR_FULL_DB, 1)*g_mh;
g_bh > 0 ? (
  gfx_set(0.980,0.640,0.200,0.85); gfx_rect(g_g1, g_my, g_mw, g_bh);
  gfx_set(1.0,0.78,0.35,1); gfx_rect(g_g1, g_my+g_bh-max(1,gk_S), g_mw, max(1,gk_S));
);

// ratio: grows UPWARD from 1:1 at the bottom to RATIO_FULL at the top, on a
// log scale that does NOT follow Max Ratio - see the constant.
g_bh = min(log(max(rt_now,1))/LN_RATIO, 1)*g_mh;
g_bh > 0 ? (
  gfx_set(0.42,0.95,0.62,0.85); gfx_rect(g_g2, g_my+g_mh-g_bh, g_mw, g_bh);
  gfx_set(0.62,1.0,0.78,1); gfx_rect(g_g2, g_my+g_mh-g_bh, g_mw, max(1,gk_S));
);

// frames last, so the fills sit inside them
gfx_set(0.28,0.28,0.32,1);
gfx_rect(g_g1, g_my, g_mw, g_mh, 0);
gfx_rect(g_g2, g_my, g_mw, g_mh, 0);

// End-of-scale captions, outside the bars: without them neither meter says
// what "full" means. Both columns sit to the LEFT of their bar and are
// RIGHT-aligned to it, so they grow into the gap instead of off the panel.
gfx_setfont(1);
gfx_set(gk_dim_r, gk_dim_g, gk_dim_b, 1);
sprintf(#s, "%d", GR_FULL_DB);
gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
gfx_x = g_g1 - 4*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my; gfx_drawstr(#s);
sprintf(#s, "0");
gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
gfx_x = g_g1 - 4*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my + g_mh - g_th; gfx_drawstr(#s);
sprintf(#s, "%d", RATIO_FULL);
gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
gfx_x = g_g2 - 4*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my; gfx_drawstr(#s);
sprintf(#s, "1");
gfx_measurestr(#s, g_tw, g_th);
gfx_x = g_g2 - 4*gk_S - g_tw; gfx_y = g_my + g_mh - g_th; gfx_drawstr(#s);

gk_text_c(876, BOT_Y + 98, "GR", 1);
gk_text_c(936, BOT_Y + 98, "RATIO", 1);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f dB", gr_db);
gk_field(848, BOT_Y + 112, 56, 16, #s);
sprintf(#s, "%.1f:1", rt_now);
gk_field(908, BOT_Y + 112, 56, 16, #s);

help_open ? gk_help_draw(10, 40, 966, 594, "Help  -  Variable Ratio Compressor MB", HELP_BASE, help_lines);

// Wordmark, bottom right, its right edge on the same 10 px margin as the
// panels. LOGO_ASPECT is the source's width/height; a 1-frame strip is just a
// scaled blit that respects gk_S. The version sits opposite it, so a stale
// compiled instance is visible at a glance - REAPER caches compiled JSFX per
// FX instance.
gk_strip(11, 1, 0, UI_W - 10 - 14*LOGO_ASPECT, UI_H - 24, 14*LOGO_ASPECT, 14);
gk_text_c(30, UI_H - 22, "v1.2", 1);

g_bad = count_missing_images(15);
g_bad > 0 ? (
  gfx_set(1, 0.42, 0.38, 1);
  gfx_x = 400*gk_S; gfx_y = 15*gk_S;
  gfx_drawstr("images failed to load: ");
  gfx_drawnumber(g_bad, 0);
);

// One flag, picked up by @block on the audio thread.
g_chg ? need_update = 1;
gk_end();

@serialize
// GUI-only state, so it is serialised rather than made a slider. Append-only:
// a new value goes AFTER the existing ones or every project saved with the
// old order reads back wrong. A project saved before a field existed simply
// runs out of data, and the variable keeps the default @init gave it.
file_var(0, gui_scale_idx);
file_var(0, curve_mask);
